  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled and its latest run finished with status `ok`.\n\n**Data exploration.** The data view `kibana_sample_data_logs` uses `@timestamp` as its time field. The fields that matter here are `response` (with a `response.keyword` subfield for exact matching), `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.dest`, `extension`, `host`, `url`, and `request`. I could not run value aggregations: the console proxy, internal search, and ES|QL endpoints are all disabled or absent on this Kibana, and Elasticsearch isn't exposed on a local port. So I didn't check the actual time range or the distribution of response codes.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-70efe73b Web logs overview`** (id `4e63b8be-629b-4bc8-a9e1-799bafbe3da2`)\n- A markdown header.\n- Three ES|QL metric panels: total requests, 5xx responses (`response.keyword LIKE \"5*\"`), and average bytes.\n- A \"Requests over time\" line chart.\n- A \"Requests by response code\" bar chart.\n- Time range: last 7 days. This is my choice, since the request didn't specify one.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-70efe73b 5xx spike`** (id `8c296338-6145-41f2-8981-083a250f17f5`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query rule, using a data-view search on `kibana_sample_data_logs` with the KQL filter `response.keyword: 5*`.\n- Schedule: every 1 minute.\n- Condition: count is greater than 10 over a 5-minute window.\n- Consumer `stackAlerts`, no actions attached. So it records alerts but sends no notifications until you add a connector.\n\nThe rule's first attempt used a Query DSL filter with `{{date_start}}`/`{{date}}` placeholders, and those failed to parse on every run. I replaced it with the KQL version, which runs cleanly. The rule was updated in place, so it's still the same object.\n\nI haven't confirmed that the 5xx filter matches any documents, since I couldn't query the index directly. The rule's run succeeding shows the query is valid, not that it finds data.",
